\begin{abstract}
Learning continuously from a single stream of experience, in real time and from
local signals, is how brains learn and what neuromorphic hardware requires, yet
very few learning rules for recurrent networks remain applicable under these
constraints. We study reinforcement learning in recurrent spiking neural networks
(SRNNs) in a \emph{strictly online} setting: updates at every timestep from
quantities local in space and time, with no replay, no stored trajectories, and a
network state that is never reset. We first show that e-prop, which
routes a per-neuron learning signal through feedback weights, learns effectively in
this setting, including in deeper networks\pending{: depth series}, provided its
feedback reaches every neuron; symmetric feedback fails as soon as hidden neurons
are not connected to the readout. We then introduce \emph{\method{}}, which needs no
feedback pathway at all: every neuron perturbs its own input, and a single broadcast
temporal-difference error is correlated with a local trace of that perturbation. The
expected update splits into a REINFORCE-like perturbation gradient and a
\emph{perturbation-induced drift}, a reward-modulated, Hebbian-like term that
controls where the network remains plastic. With a neuron-local decorrelation reward,
credited through the same noise trace, the hidden layers become a reward-guided
feature-building mechanism for an online-trained readout. E-prop, with its richer feedback, learns
faster, as expected; \method{} learns with one broadcast scalar, far closer to e-prop
than the unfavourable scaling of perturbation learning with network size would
suggest, and keeps its neurons in the plastic regime.\pending{: numbers after the
rerun with the corrected e-prop baselines.}
\end{abstract}

