\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Online learning in recurrent networks.}
The exact recurrent gradient is given by BPTT~\citep{Werbos1990BackpropagationTT},
whose memory grows with sequence length; truncation (TBPTT) bounds this at the
cost of the credit horizon~\citep{WilliamsZipser1995}, and vanishing gradients
further limit how far credit can travel~\citep{learningLongTermHard}. RTRL
computes the gradient forward in time~\citep{RTRL}, but it is non-local in
space, tracking the sensitivity of every unit to every parameter at $O(n^3)$
storage and $O(n^4)$ compute per step,
motivating cheaper forward approximations: UORO gives an unbiased low-rank
estimator~\citep{UORO}, OSTL separates spatial and temporal credit for deep
spiking networks~\citep{OSTL}, and synthetic gradients decouple modules through
predicted feedback~\citep{Jaderberg2016}. E-prop~\citep{eprop2020} is the member
of this family we build on, factorising the gradient into a forward,
synapse-local eligibility trace and a per-neuron learning signal; the rate-based
RFLO rule is a close analogue~\citep{murray2019local}, and it has recently been
used for fully online recurrent actor-critic learning from a single continuing
stream in rate networks~\citep{Lemmel2025RTRRL}. Among these methods,
e-prop is, to our knowledge, the most developed for reward-based learning in
spiking networks under forward, local constraints, which is why we use it as
the reference for the strict setting studied here. \Method{} stays
within this forward, local regime but removes the structured learning signal.

\paragraph{Credit assignment without symmetric feedback.}
Backpropagation requires symmetric feedback weights, which feedback alignment
showed to be unnecessary: fixed random feedback can still carry a usable error
signal~\citep{Lillicrap2016}, a principle extended to recurrent networks in
RFLO~\citep{murray2019local} and available in e-prop as its random and adaptive
feedback variants~\citep{eprop2020}. A more radical step drops backpropagated
feedback in favour of locally computed TD errors: \citet{guan2024artificialdopamine}
recently showed that per-layer TD errors, each computed locally and
distributed synchronously within its layer, closer to how dopamine is actually
delivered, may be sufficient to learn complex RL tasks in non-spiking deep
Q-networks. \Method{} goes further for spiking recurrent networks: it
uses no feedback matrix of any kind and a single broadcast scalar, the global
TD error, together with each neuron's local perturbation and eligibility
traces.

\paragraph{Perturbation-based and reward-modulated learning.}
Correlating stochastic perturbations with a global reward has a long history,
from stochastic error-descent~\citep{Cauwenberghs1992} and
REINFORCE~\citep{Williams1992REINFORCE} to conductance perturbation in spiking
neurons~\citep{fiete2006gradient} and modern evolution
strategies~\citep{salimans2017evolutionstrategiesscalablealternative}. In
neuroscience the same ingredients recur as three-factor, reward-modulated STDP,
where a global neuromodulator gates a local Hebbian trace
\citep{Fremaux2016NeuromodulatedSTDP,Izhikevich2007DistalReward,Florian2007RewardSTDP,Legenstein2008RewardSTDP},
and as continuous-time actor-critic learning in spiking
networks~\citep{Frmaux2013ReinforcementLU}. Node perturbation has recently
been revisited as a scalable learning rule: its variance, stability, and
scaling with network size have been analysed~\citep{Hiratani2022NodePerturbation},
and decorrelation and activity-based variants make it effective in multi-layer
networks~\citep{Dalm2026NodePerturbation}. Closest to our work,
\citet{GarciaFernandez2024RNNPerturbation} train recurrent rate networks online
with random perturbations, and \citet{GarciaFernandez2026NoiseReward} combine
node noise, eligibility traces, and a reward-prediction error for reinforcement
learning with a view to neuromorphic hardware. These rules use rate neurons,
compare each noisy run with a noise-free reference pass, and pair the noise
with presynaptic activity only; ours works in recurrent spiking networks from a
single noisy pass, credits a broadcast TD error at every timestep, and pairs a
membrane-filtered perturbation trace with e-prop's pseudo-derivative-gated
eligibility trace. That gate has theoretical support:
\citet{Gygax2025SurrogateTheory} show that surrogate derivatives correspond to
smoothed derivatives of stochastic spiking neurons, which suits a rule whose
neurons are made stochastic by exploration noise. Our rule thus sits at the
intersection of these lines: a perturbation-based policy gradient whose
credit is shaped by the e-prop eligibility trace and its surrogate
derivative~\citep{Neftci2019Surrogate}, run online and in continuous time. It
is complementary to value-based spiking RL such as spiking
Q-learning~\citep{Chen2022SpikingQLearning}.

\paragraph{Derivatives from Gaussian noise.}
Stein's identity~\unverified{Stein 1981} underlies Gaussian-smoothing
(zeroth-order) gradient estimators~\unverified{Nesterov and Spokoiny 2017;
Spall 1992}, and applied twice it gives Hessian estimates from function values
alone~\unverified{Erdogdu 2015, Newton--Stein}; random probes likewise estimate
traces of Jacobians~\unverified{Hutchinson 1989; Grathwohl et al.\ 2019, FFJORD}.
Forward-gradient methods estimate gradients along random
directions~\unverified{Baydin et al.\ 2022} and become practical when combined
with local losses~\unverified{Ren et al.\ 2023}. These methods differentiate an
objective chosen in advance. In our rule the identity acts, implicitly, on the
synapse's own eligibility trace: because the gate depends on the noise, the
perturbation that estimates the reward gradient also yields the weight derivative
of the eligibility, a local second-order term that drives most of the feature
learning (Section~\ref{sec:results-features}). To our knowledge this use of the
identity, as a plasticity term in its own right, has not been described before.
For perturbation learning itself, the comparison between weight and node
perturbation in temporally extended tasks~\unverified{Z\"uge, Klos and
Memmesheimer, weight vs.\ node perturbation in temporally extended tasks} is
relevant to our choice of noise on the transmitted weight.

