\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{A Decoupled TD($\lambda$) Update}
\label{sec:tdlambda}

One piece of supporting machinery is needed before the rules themselves. In the
standard TD($\lambda$) update the credit trace decays at $\gamma\lambda$, so a
single $\gamma$ both discounts the bootstrap target (Eq.~\eqref{eq:tderror}) and
bounds how far back a reward-prediction error is assigned. With a noisy
spike-based critic these roles conflict: $\gamma$ must be small for the value
estimate to stay learnable, but a small $\gamma$ then caps the actor's credit horizon at
$\gamma\lambda\le\gamma$, sometimes below a single action. We therefore decouple
the two, dropping $\gamma$ from the actor and hidden-weight traces so that
$\lambda$ alone sets the horizon,
\begin{equation}
p^{\,t}_{ji} = \lambda\, p^{\,t-1}_{ji} + L^t_j\,\bar e^{\,t}_{ji},
\qquad \Delta W_{ji} = -\eta\sum_t \delta_t\, p^{\,t}_{ji},
\label{eq:actor-modified}
\end{equation}
while the critic keeps $\gamma$ (equivalently, runs at $\lambda_V=1$),
\begin{equation}
\nu^{\,t}_{ji} = \gamma\,\nu^{\,t-1}_{ji} + \bar e^{\,V,t}_{ji},
\qquad \Delta W^V_{ji} = \eta_V\sum_t \delta_t\,\nu^{\,t}_{ji}.
\label{eq:critic-modified}
\end{equation}
Reinstating $\gamma$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:actor-modified} recovers standard
TD($\lambda$)~\citep{SuttonBarto2018}; dropping it is a deliberate, biased choice that preserves the
long-range credit a short $\gamma$ trace would suppress. Because each action
spans $K$ integration steps, per-step decays are
$\gamma_{\mathrm{step}}=\gamma^{1/K}$ and
$\lambda_{\mathrm{step}}=\lambda^{1/K}$; we use $\gamma=0.5$, $\lambda=0.8$
at the action level throughout.

The decoupling has a standard reading. A textbook actor trace decays by
$\gamma\lambda_{\mathrm{GAE}}$, so Eq.~\eqref{eq:actor-modified} is generalised
advantage estimation~\citep{schulman2016gae} with $\lambda=\gamma\lambda_{\mathrm{GAE}}$:
$\lambda=\gamma$ gives the Monte-Carlo advantage $G_t-V_t$, and with an accurate
critic every $\lambda$ gives an unbiased advantage estimate, trading critic bias
against return variance. Our setting ($\lambda>\gamma$) lies beyond the
Monte-Carlo limit and adds variance; we keep it because it worked best in our
experiments (Appendix~\ref{sec:results-lambda}). For the perturbation-induced drift of
Section~\ref{sec:distributed} the trace plays a different role: with an accurate
critic it is redundant in expectation, and during learning it integrates the
critic's systematic errors, which speeds up the drift
(Appendix~\ref{app:decomp-lambda}).

\subsection{Perturbation-based E-prop}
\label{sec:distributed}

\paragraph{Noise and its trace.}
Every neuron $j$ draws one Gaussian sample $n^t_j\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ per step,
and each synapse that transmits a spike at that step delivers $W_{ji}+\sigma
n^t_j$ instead of $W_{ji}$. The noise is shared by all afferents of a neuron and
explores through the spikes it shifts; it is the only source of exploration in
the hidden layers. Each synapse keeps the noise it has delivered, filtered by the
membrane decay,
\begin{equation}
\xi^t_{ji}=\alpha\,\xi^{t-1}_{ji}+\sigma\,n^t_j\,z^{t-1}_i ,
\label{eq:perturbation-trace}
\end{equation}
a local record of how the noise has moved the membrane through this synapse.

\paragraph{The update and its two components.}
The update correlates the noise trace with the TD error through the
e-prop eligibility. With $\pi^t_{ji}=\bar z^t_i-\beta\,\epsilon^{t-1}_{ji}$ the
presynaptic factor of e-prop's ALIF eligibility trace and $g^t_j$ a postsynaptic
gate, the \emph{score} of a synapse is
$s^t_{ji}=\kappa\,g^t_j\,\pi^t_{ji}\,\xi^t_{ji}/\sigma^2$, with
$\kappa=1-\alpha^2$ a normalising constant; it enters a credit trace
$p^t_{ji}=\lambda p^{t-1}_{ji}+s^t_{ji}$, and the optimiser descends on
$\sum_t\delta_t\,p^t_{ji}$. For the gate $g=\psi$, $g\pi=e$ is e-prop's
eligibility trace. For fixed weights,
$\sum_t\delta_tp^t_{ji}=\sum_t s^t_{ji}A^t$ with
$A^t=\sum_{k\ge0}\lambda^k\delta_{t+k}$, so each score is weighted by an advantage
estimate of what followed it (Section~\ref{sec:tdlambda}).

Because the noise is Gaussian, Stein's identity~\unverified{Stein 1981},
$\mathbb{E}[n\,f(n)]=\mathbb{E}[f'(n)]$, turns the mean of the score into a
derivative: $\mathbb{E}[s^t_{ji}]=\mathbb{E}[D_{ji}e^t_{ji}]$, where $D_{ji}$ is
the weight derivative seen through the kernel of the noise trace, taken through the
true dynamics of the network, including resets, adaptation and recurrence
(Appendix~\ref{app:decomp}). We call
$P^t_{ji}=\mathbb{E}[D_{ji}e^t_{ji}]$ the \emph{perturbation-induced drift} and
$Q^t_{ji}=s^t_{ji}-P^t_{ji}$, which has zero mean, the \emph{noise part}. In a
trained network the drift is the weight derivative of the eligibility trace,
$P\approx\bar c\,\partial\mathbb{E}[e]/\partial W$ ($R^2=0.83$, with $\bar c$ the
average number of afferents sharing a neuron's noise;
Appendix~\ref{app:decomp-verify}). Applying the lemma to the product with the
advantage separates the expected update into two terms,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\big[A^t s^t_{ji}\big]=
\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\big[e^t_{ji}\,D_{ji}A^t\big]}_{\text{perturbation gradient}}
+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\big[A^t\,D_{ji}e^t_{ji}\big]}_{\text{perturbation-induced drift}} .
\label{eq:two-terms}
\end{equation}
In estimator terms, one Gaussian sample thus yields two estimates: a
score-function (REINFORCE) estimate of the reward gradient, and a zeroth-order
estimate of a second-order quantity, the weight derivative of the eligibility
(Section~\ref{sec:related}). We found no other way to compute this quantity at a
comparable cost. Through the spiking dynamics the derivative is defined only after
smoothing by the noise, which leaves sampling as the only general method. On a
surrogate-differentiable model it requires the diagonal of the network's influence
matrix, which costs as much as RTRL, and the deterministic local
approximation we tested captures only part of it
(Appendix~\ref{app:drift-cost}).
The \emph{perturbation gradient}, carried by $Q$, is the REINFORCE
principle~\citep{Williams1992REINFORCE}: it weights the sensitivity of the
subsequent TD errors to the weight by the eligibility, and is the term that
improves the policy. The \emph{perturbation-induced drift}, carried by $P$, weights
the sensitivity of the eligibility to the weight by the outcome. Since the
optimiser descends, each synapse descends its \emph{own} outcome-weighted eligibility,
$\sum_t\mathrm{sg}(A^t)\,e^t_{ji}$, with respect to its own weight (with
$\mathrm{sg}$ marking a fixed modulator). The synapses thus play a game, each with
its own cost, rather than descending a shared objective, and the joint update is not
the gradient of any scalar function: its Jacobian is significantly asymmetric, and
no positive per-synapse rescaling of the learning rates makes it a gradient
(Appendix~\ref{app:drift-nongradient}; simultaneous gradient play in differentiable
games behaves the same way~\unverified{Balduzzi et al.\ 2018, The mechanics of
$n$-player differentiable games}). We therefore describe the drift by what it does:
it moves eligibility, the sensitivity and plasticity of a synapse's neuron, away from
moments followed by better-than-expected outcomes, freezing those responses, and
towards moments followed by worse ones, keeping them plastic. It is a three-factor Hebbian
term: a presynaptic trace times a postsynaptic factor that is positive while the
neuron is depolarised below threshold and negative at and just after a spike,
modulated by the TD error (Appendix~\ref{app:hebb}; the sign pattern resembles
voltage-based plasticity~\citep{Clopath2010Voltage}). The drift builds the
features that both readouts need, and the perturbation gradient improves the
policy on top of them (Section~\ref{sec:results-snake}).

\paragraph{The rule.}
Descending on $\sum_tA^ts^t$ descends on both terms of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:two-terms}. That is the right direction for the drift but the wrong
one for the gradient, which must be ascended. We therefore change only the gate.
To first order the noise part is the score evaluated with a gate that does not
depend on the current noise, $Q\approx\kappa\,\psi_0\,\pi\,\xi/\sigma^2$, so
replacing $\psi$ by $\psi-2\psi_0$ turns $P+Q$ into $P-Q$. We estimate the
noise-free gate by its running average,
$\bar\psi^t_j=\beta_\psi\bar\psi^{t-1}_j+(1-\beta_\psi)\psi^t_j$ with
$\beta_\psi=0.99$ per step, which gives the fully local, online update
\begin{equation}
\Delta W_{ji}\;\propto\;-\sum_t \delta_t\;\mathcal{F}_{\lambda}\!\left(
\kappa\,\big(\psi^t_j-2\bar\psi^t_j\big)\,\pi^t_{ji}\,
\frac{\xi^t_{ji}}{\sigma^2}\right),
\label{eq:distributed-update}
\end{equation}
with $\mathcal{F}_\lambda$ the credit filter of Section~\ref{sec:tdlambda} and the
minus sign that of the optimiser. Antithetic measurements confirm that the noise
part of this rule has the REINFORCE sign (Appendix~\ref{app:gridtoy-decomp}). An
earlier variant with the gate $\psi$, which we found empirically and which
reverses the perturbation gradient, learns through the drift alone; it is
analysed in Appendix~\ref{app:original}.

\paragraph{Neuron-local rewards: decorrelation.}
Because the score correlates a neuron's own noise with whatever reward it is
multiplied by, a reward computed inside the neuron can be credited exactly like the
broadcast TD error: each synapse correlates its noise trace with the neuron's local
reward minus a running baseline, and adds the result to its update. We use one such
reward. The network has excitatory (E) and inhibitory (I) populations, and all
connections from the I population arrive on a separate input channel, so that every
neuron sees the input it receives from that population, $I^{\mathrm{inh}}_j$. The
populations are defined by their initial weights (Appendix~\ref{app:arch}): the rule
does not, for now, keep each weight's sign, so a synapse from an I neuron can become
excitatory during training. $I^{\mathrm{inh}}_j$ is therefore the input from the
neurons \emph{labelled} inhibitory, and it means inhibition only as long as most of
these synapses keep their initial sign. This is not guaranteed: in a five-layer run
the fraction of I$\to$E synapses with an excitatory weight rose from below $1\%$ to
$23$--$35\%$ per layer over $350{,}000$ moves, and was still rising slowly, while the
feedforward relay between layers kept its signs.\pending{: whether the I channel stays
net inhibitory (weight-weighted), and more seeds.} The local reward
\begin{equation}
r^{\mathrm{dec}}_{j,t}=-c_D\,\frac{(z^t_j-\bar z^t_j)\,(I^{\mathrm{inh},t}_j-\bar I^{\mathrm{inh},t}_j)}{\bar I^{\mathrm{inh},t}_j}
\label{eq:decorr}
\end{equation}
penalises spiking in step with that inhibition, with running means $\bar z_j$ and
$\bar I^{\mathrm{inh}}_j$ over about $1$\,s. Since the inhibitory neurons pool the
activity of the excitatory neurons that drive them, firing together with the received
inhibition means firing together with one's neighbours, and the reward pushes each neuron
towards responses that its neighbourhood does not already provide. It uses only the
neuron's own spikes and input, so the rule stays local. This reading rests on the
I population staying inhibitory: the normalisation by $\bar I^{\mathrm{inh}}_j$ assumes
a net inhibitory channel, and if enough of its synapses change sign the denominator
approaches zero or changes sign and the reward loses its meaning. Enforcing the signs
(Dale's law), for instance by pruning and rewiring synapses that change sign, would keep
the meaning fixed; we leave it optional here, since in a first test enforcing it on all
connections of a deep network made learning fail\pending{: Dale restricted to
I$\to$E connections}. We use $c_D=1$, the uncentred
drift and no homeostat: the uncentred drift's bias wakes initially quiet neurons, and
the homeostat, which pulls every neuron towards a fixed sensitivity, works against the
decorrelation reward (below).\pending{: decorrelation results, 1 and 5 layers; firing
rates.}

\paragraph{An alternative: centring and the homeostat.}
Without the decorrelation reward, two other additions keep the drift useful in long
runs. First, the drift is modulated by the
TD error minus its running mean, $\delta_t-\bar\delta_t$. While the critic is biased,
$\mathbb{E}[\delta]\neq0$, and the uncentred drift contains a term
$\mathbb{E}[\delta]\,\mathbb{E}[P]$ that pushes the sensitivity of every neuron in the
same direction, whatever the outcome; centring removes it and leaves the covariance
that allocates plasticity. The gradient part is not centred: its score has zero mean,
so the running mean acts only as a baseline there. Neither is any critic update, which
needs the bias to correct itself. Second, a homeostat adds
$c_H\,\overline{|\delta|}\,(\bar\psi_j-\psi^*)$ times the drift trace, which pulls each
neuron's average sensitivity $\bar\psi_j$ towards a target $\psi^*$: the drift trace
supplies the direction in which the weights change $\bar\psi_j$, and the scale
$\overline{|\delta|}$ keeps the term's strength relative to the reward-driven ones. The
bias term of the uncentred drift is not only a nuisance: early in training it is what
wakes up initially quiet neurons, so centring is used together with the homeostat,
which replaces it in a controlled form (Section~\ref{sec:results-features}). Both
additions are local, since $\bar\delta$ and $\overline{|\delta|}$ are running
statistics of the broadcast scalar. We use $c_H=1$ and $\psi^*=0.1$. The homeostat and
the decorrelation reward compete: the first holds every neuron at a fixed average
sensitivity, while the second rewards a neuron for not firing with its neighbours,
whatever that does to its sensitivity; combined, they fight over the same neurons, and in our
runs the decorrelation reward alone was the more useful of the two.\pending{: the
comparison runs.}

\paragraph{The role of the gate.}
The pseudo-derivative is borrowed from e-prop, but here it is structural rather
than a surrogate: the drift is the sensitivity of the gate to the neuron's noise,
so without a gate that depends on the postsynaptic state ($g=1$) the drift
vanishes and only the perturbation gradient remains. Its width sets where the
drift acts. A gate narrower than the default concentrates the drift at threshold
and destabilises the activity, while widths at or above $v_{\mathrm{th}}$ work
equally well in exploratory runs (Appendix~\ref{app:gridtoy-exploratory}).

\paragraph{Why noise.}
In the deterministic network the derivative of the eligibility is degenerate: it
vanishes almost everywhere and is impulsive where a weight change moves a spike,
and these spike and reset events carry most of the drift. The noise smooths them:
the drift is the derivative of the Gaussian-smoothed dynamics, as in zeroth-order
optimisation~\unverified{Nesterov and Spokoiny 2017; Spall 1992 (SPSA)}, taken
through the whole network using only each synapse's own noise trace. The drift is nevertheless a property
of the eligibility, not of the noise: a local deterministic surrogate built from
e-prop's quantities, the slope of the gate and a kernel for how a spike closes and
resets it, already captures much of it ($R^2\approx0.56$;
Appendix~\ref{app:decomp-verify}). Computing the drift without noise, a second-order
counterpart of e-prop, is an open problem, but there is little practical reason to
prefer it: the same noise trace provides both terms, and estimating the drift from
it needs only a neuron's own noise. Its signal-to-noise ratio changes little when the
noise on all other neurons is switched on ($0.88$ times), whereas that of the
perturbation gradient for the same neurons falls to $0.38$ times, because its
estimate depends on the response of the whole network
(Appendix~\ref{app:decomp-locality}).

\paragraph{No gradient chains.}
The update never multiplies derivatives along a path. BPTT and RTRL chain
sensitivities through time and through the network, and a product of many factors
tends to vanish or explode. Both components here are instead estimated by a single
correlation between a synapse's own noise trace and a local quantity, the
eligibility for the drift and the broadcast outcome for the gradient, however long
or deep the path from that synapse to the reward. Their magnitude is set by the
neuron's own noise and the shared outcome rather than by a product of
sensitivities, so path length and delay affect their precision, through added
noise, but not their magnitude. The only signals propagated in time are per-neuron
or per-synapse traces with decay factors below one ($\alpha$, $\rho$, $\beta_\psi$
and $\lambda$), so long-range credit fades through a deliberate, tunable horizon
rather than through products of Jacobians. E-prop shares this property; the two
differ in how the magnitude is set per neuron. In e-prop it scales with that
neuron's feedback weights; in \method{} it is set by the neuron's own noise and the
shared outcome, and so is comparable across neurons.

\paragraph{Where to inject the noise (variants).}
The shared noise of Eq.~\eqref{eq:perturbation-trace} (\variant{weight dist}) is
our default. Two alternatives change the drift. Independent noise per synapse
(\variant{weight dist ind noise}, generated as a hash of the neuron's noise, the
synapse indices and time) removes the multiplicity $c_j$ of afferents sharing a
neuron's noise, which scales the drift ($P\approx\bar c\,\partial e/\partial W$),
and learns less well. Perturbing the membrane directly (\variant{node dist}) uses
one neuron-level trace $\xi^t_j=\alpha\xi^{t-1}_j+\sigma n^t_j$, whose kernel weights
all presynaptic activity equally rather than through the synapse's own spikes. Neuron-local objectives, such as voltage stability or a firing-rate
reward, can be credited through the same trace exactly like the broadcast TD error;
apart from the decorrelation reward, the main experiments do not use them
(Appendix~\ref{app:gridtoy-exploratory}).

\paragraph{Backward propagation (optional).}
Each neuron's gated noise score is also a local estimate of its contribution to the
outcome, and it can be sent backward through the forward weights: every
hidden-to-hidden synapse adds its weight times its postsynaptic neuron's score to its
presynaptic neuron, which uses the sum as an e-prop-style learning signal. No feedback
pathway is added, since each signal travels back through the synapse that carries the
forward one. With membrane noise, whose score is neuron-level, this improved learning
on a small network\pending{: CPU, 2 seeds, reward rate $-0.028$ vs.\ $-0.069$;
full-size runs}; with the per-synapse noise of \variant{weight dist}, each synapse sends
only its share of the noise and the signal is biased. It is not used in the main
experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:routing}).\todo{Update Appendix~\ref{app:routing} with
the backward-propagation results.}

\subsection{Optimisation and Regularisation}
\label{sec:optimiser}

Section~\ref{sec:intro} argues that updating every timestep is the setting
most faithful to a continuously learning agent; this section addresses the
cost of doing so.
Because updates are applied every timestep from a single local sample, that sample
is often noisy. All rules use AdaBelief~\citep{zhuang2020adabelief} with
$\beta_1=0.99$, $\beta_2=0.99999$, whose second-moment estimate tracks the variance of
the gradient around its own momentum rather than its raw magnitude, which gave smoother
updates under strict per-timestep updating.\pending{: confirm for all e-prop baselines
after the rerun.} All rules are updated at every simulation timestep, the strict
schedule of Section~\ref{sec:intro}, with no exceptions.

To counter the loss of plasticity that continual, per-timestep updating can
otherwise induce, every rule additionally regularises its weights toward
their values at initialisation, an approach known as \emph{L2
Init}~\citep{kumar2023maintaining}: an $\ell_2$ penalty
$c_{\mathrm{init}}\lVert\theta^t-\theta^0\rVert_2^2$ on the deviation of the
current parameters $\theta^t$ from their initial values $\theta^0$ is added
to the loss at every update, with $c_{\mathrm{init}}=10^{-5}$ throughout,
discouraging unbounded weight growth without constraining where the network
is allowed to move relative to its starting point.
