\subsection{What the perturbation rule learns}
\label{sec:results-features}

\paragraph{Features.} If the drift and the perturbation gradient build useful features,
the hidden representation should come to encode task variables that the reservoir's
fixed representation does not. We record the hidden state (membrane potentials and
refractory state) at the start and at the end of training and fit linear decoders for
the position of the food relative to the head, the danger in each direction, and the
heading.\pending{: decoding accuracies for \method{}, e-prop and the reservoir.}

\paragraph{Keeping neurons plastic.} A neuron whose membrane stays far from threshold
has $\psi=0$ and can no longer learn under any of the rules in this paper.\pending{:
dead-neuron counts with the decorrelation reward.} With the
homeostat, \method{} keeps essentially all of its neurons in the plastic regime, whereas
feedback-based e-prop drives a large part of its membranes outside it (voltage loss
$\approx2.4$ against $\approx0.13$)\pending{: per-population dead-neuron counts}. The
centred drift alone is not enough: on a small network, about half of the readout-field
neurons stayed silent from the start of training, since centring also removes the early,
bias-driven push that wakes them up; with the homeostat no neuron died
(Section~\ref{sec:distributed}).\pending{: small-network result, 2 seeds.}

\paragraph{Mechanism.} Removing the drift (\variant{gradient only}) or the perturbation
gradient (\variant{drift only}) isolates the two parts of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:two-terms}.\pending{: Snake dissection.} In a network-level toy task the
drift builds the features both readouts use, and without it neurons lose their
plasticity (Appendix~\ref{app:gridtoy}).

\paragraph{Scaling.} The variance of perturbation-based gradient estimates grows with
the number of perturbed units \citep{Werfel2005LearningCurves}, so if \method{} were a
gradient estimator alone, its gap to e-prop should widen with network size.\pending{:
gap against the number of hidden neurons.}

