\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Under strictly online constraints, e-prop with feedback that reaches every
neuron learns effectively\pending{: and in deeper networks}. Its per-neuron, structured
learning signal makes it the faster learner, as the amount of information it delivers
would suggest. \method{} shows that the feedback pathway itself is not required: one
broadcast scalar and each neuron's own noise are enough to train the hidden layers of a
recurrent spiking network in this setting.

That this works at all is the less expected part. Perturbation-based gradient
estimates become noisier as more units are perturbed
\citep{Werfel2005LearningCurves,fiete2006gradient}, and with thousands of neurons a
pure estimator would learn very slowly. The perturbation-induced drift is not such an
estimator. It is a local, reward-modulated rule over the eligibility, estimated from a
neuron's own noise with a signal-to-noise ratio that changes little when every other
neuron is perturbed as well (Appendix~\ref{app:decomp-locality}). It decides where the
network remains plastic, freezing responses that precede better-than-expected outcomes
and sensitising those that precede worse ones, and in doing so builds features that the
readout can use. It is not the gradient of any objective
(Appendix~\ref{app:drift-nongradient}), so this functional description, together with
the measured features (Section~\ref{sec:results-snake}), is the account we can give of
it. The rule thus sits between reservoir computing, with fixed features and
a trained readout, and end-to-end gradient learning: the features are shaped by reward,
without a gradient being transported to them.

The way the drift is computed also makes the rule less tied to the neuron model. Its
derivative is taken by the dynamics themselves: the noise enters the input current, and
Stein's lemma turns its correlation with the eligibility into the weight derivative of
that eligibility through whatever the neuron does, including resets, adaptation and
refractoriness (Appendix~\ref{app:decomp-def}). The same holds for the perturbation
gradient. Applying the rule to a variation of the neuron model, or to a different model,
therefore requires only injecting the noise and keeping its trace. E-prop instead
derives its eligibility traces from the state equations of each model, and must replace
every non-differentiable step with a pseudo-derivative. Each new model needs a new
derivation, and the approximation keeps only the terms that derivation retains. Two parts
of our rule remain model-specific: the gate and the presynaptic factor of the score,
which we borrow from e-prop's ALIF eligibility, and the membrane kernel of the noise
trace. Since the drift differentiates whatever eligibility the rule computes, these are
design choices rather than derived quantities. We have only tested the ALIF model, so
this is a property of the method, not yet a result. The natural targets are neurons whose
dynamics are hard to differentiate or not known at all. Analog neuromorphic hardware has
device mismatch, and gradient training there relies on a software model of the
chip~\unverified{Schmitt et al.\ 2017; Cramer et al.\ 2022}, whereas the drift
differentiates through whatever the device does. The rule does need the injected noise
itself, since Stein's lemma holds for the exact sample: a device would draw it from a
local random number generator, and its intrinsic noise would add variance but no bias.
Replacing the injected noise with the deviation of activity from its running average, as
reward-modulated Hebbian rules do~\unverified{Legenstein et al.\ 2010; Miconi 2017},
would remove this requirement at the cost of an approximation. Dendritic neurons with plateau
potentials and dendritic spikes, and synapses with short-term plasticity, would each need
new eligibility derivations for e-prop, but only the injected noise here. The cost is
signal-to-noise for slow state variables, whose effect a noise trace must integrate over
long windows.

E-prop with a feedback pathway learned faster and built better features in every network we
tried, including the deeper ones. We therefore do not present the perturbation rule as a
better learner for these networks. Its value lies in what it requires and what it computes. It
needs only a broadcast scalar and each neuron's own noise, and its main term, the weight
derivative of the eligibility, is taken through the neuron's true dynamics, which no other
method we know of computes at a comparable cost. This suggests that it should apply in more
settings than e-prop: neuron models without a convenient derivation, dendritic or synaptic
dynamics, and hardware whose dynamics are not known. In all of these, e-prop needs a
model-specific derivation or a model of the device. Within the settings studied here, it is
best understood as a reward-modulated reservoir-shaping rule. We have not found a way to turn
these properties into an advantage over e-prop, and doing so is the main open question this
work leaves.

The same handle on plasticity makes the rule robust in a way that feedback-based rules
are not. Neurons whose membranes leave the range of the pseudo-derivative stop learning
under any of the rules studied here. Because the noise trace credits any neuron-local
reward, the rule has a direct handle on this: a homeostat keeps neurons in range, and
the decorrelation reward, which worked better in our runs, also keeps neurons from
duplicating their neighbours. Loss of plasticity is a central problem of continual
learning \citep{kumar2023maintaining}, and local rewards credited through the
neuron's own noise are a natural tool for it.\pending{: support with the dead-neuron counts and, if they hold, task-switch results.}

Read biologically, \method{} is a three-factor rule with a neuromodulator-like broadcast
signal, whose drift has the sign structure of voltage-based plasticity
\citep{Clopath2010Voltage}. It predicts, as a hypothesis, that neurons become more
sensitive, and so more plastic, before outcomes that turn out worse than expected.

The two families need not compete. E-prop supplies a directed gradient where its
feedback reaches, and the perturbation parts can regulate plasticity on top of it;
how to combine them most effectively is an open question\pending{: e-prop with the
decorrelation reward and the drift}.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Seeds and hyperparameter search were limited and shared across rules, so comparisons are
read as existence and relational claims. All rules settle at firing rates far above the
regulariser's target, plausibly because the reactive tasks demand near-constant
firing.\pending{: firing-rate range after the rerun.}

During development we found the perturbation rule to be considerably more sensitive than
e-prop to the network architecture and activity regime. Without the searched
connectivity\pending{: reference to the architecture-search section once written} and the
neuron-local objectives it either ran away to high firing rates or learned slowly, whereas
e-prop learned on every architecture we tried, once its critic term was restricted to the
value population. We attribute this to the rule's nature: it shapes the representation the
architecture provides but receives no task gradient that could compensate for a poor one.
Architecture search was therefore part of making the rule work, not an independent
optimisation.

The softmax policy head still
samples a discrete action at fixed action-window boundaries
(Appendix~\ref{app:impl}), requiring the network to consult an implicit
external clock, and the policy-gradient term of the learning signal is
delivered only at those sampling steps (in \method{}, only to the
output weights). A genuinely clock-free agent would instead need a
continuously-defined action head, e.g.\ independent per-action Gaussian units
updated every timestep. We attempted many continuous action head
configurations, but found it substantially harder to stabilise than the
discrete softmax head. We suspect two properties of the softmax head, absent from independent
continuous units, may contribute to this gap. First, softmax's shared
normalisation couples the $A$ action logits' gradients into a single
mutually-exclusive distribution, so increasing one action's probability
mechanically decreases the others; independent action units carry no such
coupling and must learn any comparative structure among actions purely from
separate, per-unit signals. Second, the softmax head only requires a learning
signal at the specific timesteps an action is actually sampled, whereas a
continuously-defined head must be assigned credit at every timestep,
substantially increasing the surface over which credit must be correctly
apportioned. We leave a working continuous-action formulation to future work. The two rules depend on the head in different ways. E-prop's hidden learning
signal is built from the head's score function, so it needs an explicit,
differentiable policy distribution, and its quality follows that construction.
\Method{}'s hidden layers need only the scalar TD error: their signal is
defined for any head, including one that defines no distribution at all (an
argmax over noisy readouts, with exploration supplied by the network's own
noise).\pending{: noise-argmax readout result.} Its quality, however, still
inherits that of the heads: the critic is its only teacher, and the features
are refined as the actor improves.
More broadly, the action window, and the per-action discount and trace decays
defined on it, are task-specific configuration that the learning rule could in
principle take over; moving as much of it as possible into the rule, towards a
fully task-agnostic agent, is the natural next step. The episode boundaries are
the clearest example: the learner is told where a game ends, and resets its
eligibility traces and treats the final state as terminal. What the reset supplies is
\emph{relevance}: the point after which past activity no longer predicts the TD
errors to come. A surprise is not such a point (it is precisely when credit
must reach the past), but relevance can be measured locally after the fact.
One route keeps a short and a long eligibility trace per synapse and weights the
long one by how consistent its extra credit has been, deferring the decision of
what to forget instead of predicting it; another learns a state-dependent
$\lambda$ from that hindsight. Either would remove this last piece of task
structure; the rule itself needs no change for non-episodic tasks, where the
traces are simply never reset.
% ===========================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Very few learning rules can train recurrent spiking networks under strictly online
constraints. E-prop is one of them, and with feedback that reaches every
neuron it learns effectively in this setting\pending{: including deeper networks}. We
introduced \method{}, which needs no feedback pathway: a broadcast TD error and each
neuron's own perturbation trace train the hidden layers, through a perturbation gradient
and a perturbation-induced drift that controls where the network remains plastic. It
learns more slowly than e-prop, as its far poorer signal would predict, yet much closer
to it than the scaling of perturbation learning suggests, and it keeps its neurons
plastic. Removing the remaining task-specific timing, above all the fixed action window,
and combining feedback-based gradients with perturbation-based control of plasticity are
the natural next steps.

