\subsection{Limitations}
\label{app:trace-limits}

The analysis concerns a single neuron with a synthetic reward and no critic;
the reward is credited through the actor's trace but not through a learned TD
error. It measures how well each update estimates the gradient, not how well a
network learns with it, and it does not reproduce the network-level effects of
Section~\ref{sec:results-snake}, where removing $\psi$ breaks learning.


\section{Decomposition of the Perturbation Update}
\label{app:decomp}

This appendix defines the two components of the perturbation update precisely,
derives them from the update as implemented, and verifies the definition
numerically. All analyses of Appendices~\ref{app:decomp}--\ref{app:hebb} are
reproduced by documented scripts with tests in
\texttt{analysis/perturbation\_drift}.\todo{Replace the repository path by the public
code link before submission.}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{app:decomp-setup}
For the weight variants, every neuron $j$ draws one Gaussian sample
$n^t_j\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ per step, and each synapse $ji$ that transmits a
spike at that step delivers $W_{ji}+\sigma n^t_j$ instead of $W_{ji}$. The noise
is therefore shared by all afferents of a neuron and reaches the membrane as an
extra input current $\sigma n^t_j c^t_j$, where
$c^t_j=\sum_i z^{t-1}_i$ is the number of afferents that spiked. Each synapse
keeps the noise it has delivered, filtered by the membrane decay,
\begin{equation}
\xi^t_{ji}=\alpha\,\xi^{t-1}_{ji}+\sigma\,n^t_j\,z^{t-1}_i
=\sigma\sum_{k\ge0}\alpha^k z^{t-k-1}_i\,n^{t-k}_j ,
\label{eq:app-xi}
\end{equation}
and the update correlates the \emph{score}
$s^t_{ji}=\kappa\,g^t_j\,\pi^t_{ji}\,\xi^t_{ji}/\sigma^2$ with the TD error through the
credit trace of Section~\ref{sec:tdlambda}. Here
$\pi^t_{ji}=\bar z^t_i-\beta\,\epsilon^{t-1}_{ji}$ is the presynaptic factor of
e-prop's ALIF eligibility trace and $g^t_j$ is a postsynaptic gate; with
$g^t_j=\psi^t_j$, $g^t_j\pi^t_{ji}=e^t_{ji}$ is e-prop's eligibility trace.
The constant $\kappa=1-\alpha^2$ only normalises the score's scale, so that one
learning rate serves all terms of the update; it is carried along unchanged below
and omitted where it does not matter. The optimiser descends on $\sum_t\delta_t\,p^t_{ji}$ with
$p^t_{ji}=\lambda\,p^{t-1}_{ji}+s^t_{ji}$. For fixed weights,
\begin{equation}
\sum_t\delta_t\,p^t_{ji}=\sum_t s^t_{ji}\,A^t,\qquad
A^t=\sum_{k\ge0}\lambda^k\delta_{t+k},
\label{eq:app-forward}
\end{equation}
so every score is weighted by the $\lambda$-weighted sum of the TD errors that
follow it.

\subsection{The drift and the noise part}
\label{app:decomp-def}
Stein's lemma, $\mathbb{E}[n\,f(n)]=\mathbb{E}[\partial f/\partial n]$ for
$n\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)$, applies to each $n^{t-k}_j$ in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:app-xi}. Because $z^{t-k-1}_i$ precedes $n^{t-k}_j$ it does not
depend on it, and because the noise enters the dynamics only through the input
current, $\partial/\partial n^{\tau}_j=\sigma c^\tau_j\,\partial/\partial
I^\tau_j$. With $e=g\pi$ the eligibility actually computed,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\big[s^t_{ji}\big]=\mathbb{E}\big[D_{ji}\,e^t_{ji}\big],\qquad
D_{ji}=\sum_{k\ge0}\alpha^k c^{t-k}_j z^{t-k-1}_i\,
\frac{\partial}{\partial I^{t-k}_j},
\label{eq:app-stein}
\end{equation}
exactly, where the expectation is over the noise given everything else and the
derivative is taken through the true dynamics, including reset, threshold
adaptation, refractoriness and the recurrent network. $D_{ji}$ is the weight
derivative $\partial/\partial W_{ji}=\sum_k z^{t-k-1}_i\,\partial/\partial
I^{t-k}_j$ seen through the kernel of the noise trace: the same presynaptic
factor, with the membrane window $\alpha^k$ and the multiplicity $c_j$ of the
shared noise. We define the \emph{drift} and the \emph{noise part} of the score
as
\begin{equation}
P^t_{ji}=\mathbb{E}\big[D_{ji}\,e^t_{ji}\big],\qquad
Q^t_{ji}=s^t_{ji}-P^t_{ji},\quad \mathbb{E}[Q^t_{ji}]=0 .
\label{eq:app-PQ}
\end{equation}
The drift does not require $e$ to be a correct derivative of anything: it is the
noise-smoothed sensitivity of whatever eligibility the rule computes, evaluated
through the true dynamics. Applying the lemma to the product $A^t s^t_{ji}$
gives the product rule
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\big[A^t s^t_{ji}\big]=
\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\big[e^t_{ji}\,D_{ji}A^t\big]}_{\text{gradient term}}
+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\big[A^t\,D_{ji}e^t_{ji}\big]}_{\text{drift term}} .
\label{eq:app-product}
\end{equation}
The gradient term weights the sensitivity of the subsequent TD errors to the
weight by the eligibility; it is carried by $Q$ (up to a covariance term that
vanishes when the fluctuations of $D_{ji}e$ are uncorrelated with $A$). The drift
term weights the sensitivity of the eligibility to the weight by the outcome; it
is carried by $P$. An update that descends on $\sum_t A^t s^t$, as the optimiser
does, descends on both terms, i.e.\ it performs gradient descent (along $D$) on
$\mathbb{E}[\sum_t A^t e^t]$; its gradient term then points against the return.
The proposed rule keeps the drift and reverses the gradient term by changing the
gate to $g=\psi-2G$, where $G$ is an estimate of the gate that does not depend on
the current noise. To first order the noise part is $Q\approx
e_0\,\xi/\sigma^2$, with $e_0$ the eligibility at the noise-free gate, so
$(\psi-2G)\,\pi\,\xi/\sigma^2\approx P-Q$. We use an exponential moving average,
$G=\bar\psi^t_j=\beta_\psi\bar\psi^{t-1}_j+(1-\beta_\psi)\psi^t_j$ with
$\beta_\psi=0.99$ per step.

