\subsection{Computing the drift without noise}
\label{app:drift-cost}
The drift is $\mathbb{E}[D_{ji}e^t_{ji}]$, a noise-smoothed weight derivative of a
quantity that already contains a derivative (the gate), taken through the true
dynamics. We found no way to compute it other than the one the rule uses. With $N$
neurons, $S\approx Nk$ synapses of fan-in $k$, and $T$ steps:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Through the spiking dynamics.} Threshold, reset and refractoriness make
$e$ a discontinuous function of $W$, so its weight derivative is not defined
pointwise; only the smoothed expectation of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app-stein} is. Computing
that expectation exactly means integrating over all noise paths, which is
exponential in $NT$. The general method left is Monte Carlo over rollouts from the
same state, as in the verification above ($3000$ rollouts per state).
\item \emph{On a surrogate-differentiable model} (the pseudo-derivative $\psi$ in
place of the spike derivative). Here $\partial e_{ji}/\partial W_{ji}$ needs
$\partial V_j/\partial W_{ji}$ and $\partial z_i/\partial W_{ji}$ through the
recurrence, i.e.\ the diagonal of the full influence matrix. A Jacobian diagonal has
no cheaper exact form than the full forward pass: RTRL~\citep{RTRL} needs $O(NS)$
memory and $O(NSk)$ time per step ($O(N^4)$ for dense connectivity), and per-weight
tangents or backward passes need $O(S^2)$ or $O(TS^2)$ per step. The result is
moreover the derivative of the surrogate, not of the true dynamics.
\item \emph{Truncated to the neuron's own paths.} Keeping only the effect of
$W_{ji}$ on neuron $j$ itself gives a deterministic $O(S)$ rule: the deterministic
surrogate of Table~\ref{tab:verify}, which explains $56\%$ of the variance of the
drift. Adding paths through $n$-step neighbours, as in sparse approximations of
RTRL~\unverified{Menick et al.\ 2021, SnAp}, costs about $O(Sk^n)$ until the
influence matrix becomes dense.
\item \emph{A fitted local form.} The factorisation $P_{ji}\approx h_ju_{ji}$ of
Appendix~\ref{app:hebb} reproduces $84\%$ of the accumulated drift by cosine
similarity, but it is fitted, not derived.
\end{itemize}
The injected noise instead acts as a random probe of the Jacobian diagonal, in the
manner of stochastic trace estimators~\unverified{Hutchinson 1989}, applied
through the true dynamics by Stein's lemma: it costs $O(S)$ per step, the cost of
the forward pass, and is unbiased for the smoothed quantity, including resets,
adaptation and recurrence. Because the noise is shared per postsynaptic neuron,
each probe mixes the afferents that are active at the same time, which is the
origin of the factor $\bar c$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:app-P-approx}.

\subsection{Locality of the drift estimate}
\label{app:decomp-locality}
Estimating the drift involves only a neuron's own noise and eligibility, whereas the
perturbation gradient depends on how the whole network's response moves the
outcome. We tested this by keeping noise on three target neurons and switching it
on for all other neurons, from a fixed state of three trained networks (the three
neurons whose noise most detectably moves the value output; $8000$ and $20000$
rollouts). With all neurons perturbed, the signal-to-noise ratio of the drift
estimate for the targets' afferent synapses is $0.88$ times its value with only the
targets perturbed (per network $1.15$, $0.76$, $0.72$), while that of the
perturbation gradient for the same neurons falls to $0.38$ times ($0.62$, $0.34$,
$0.18$).

\subsection{The drift is not a gradient}
\label{app:drift-nongradient}
In expectation the drift of synapse $a$ is $F_a=\bar c\,\partial_a\mathbb{E}[e_a]$:
the derivative of its own eligibility along its own weight. A smooth field is the
gradient of a scalar function exactly when its Jacobian $J_{ab}=\partial_bF_a$ is
symmetric (equality of mixed partial derivatives), and here
$J_{ab}-J_{ba}=\bar c\,\partial_a\partial_b\,\mathbb{E}[e_a-e_b]$, which nothing in the
rule forces to vanish. If instead $F=D\nabla f$ for positive per-synapse learning
rates $D=\mathrm{diag}(d_a)$, then $J_{ab}/d_a=J_{ba}/d_b$ near a point where
$\nabla f$ is small, and the product
$(J_{ab}/J_{ba})(J_{bc}/J_{cb})(J_{ca}/J_{ac})$ equals one around every triangle of
synapses, whatever $D$ is.

We estimate both quantities in a recurrent network of three ALIF neurons with the
Snake neuron parameters and four Poisson inputs at $40$\,Hz ($18$ weights; firing
rates $22$, $35$ and $6$\,Hz), with static Gaussian weight noise per sample
(the Gaussian-smoothing view) and $\bar c$ a positive constant. The second-order
Stein identity, $\mathbb{E}[\xi_a\xi_b f(w+\xi)]=\sigma^4\,\partial_a\partial_b\mathbb{E}[f]$
for $a\neq b$, estimates $J_{ab}$ directly from simulated eligibilities ($400{,}000$
antithetic samples of $500$\,ms, with the noise-free run on the same input as a
control variate; error bars from $40$ independent chunks). As a null, the same
estimator with noise unrelated to the simulation has expectation exactly zero.

The null gives $1$ of $153$ synapse pairs with an asymmetry $|z|>3$, as expected by
chance; the drift gives $77$ of $153$, with or without $\bar\psi$ tracking the
weights. Asymmetric pairs are most common when a recurrent synapse is involved ($54$ of
$87$), and for pairs on different neurons the asymmetry is as large as the symmetric
part: one weight changes the other's drift, but not the reverse. Of the $37$
triangles whose six entries are all well determined ($|z|>4$), $8$ violate the
triangle condition at $99\%$ (bootstrap over chunks), against $0.4$ expected by
chance, so no per-synapse rescaling turns the drift into a gradient either. The
antisymmetric part is nevertheless small next to the diagonal (Frobenius norms $213$
and $3100$), and all eigenvalues of $J$ are real at this point, so the asymmetry does
not create local rotation; $16$ of the $18$ diagonal entries are negative, so for
$\bar c>0$ most synapses limit their own drift. Script:
\texttt{examples/eprop/drift\_curl\_test.py}.\todo{Replace the repository path by the
public one.}

\subsection{Estimating the two parts in a network}
\label{app:decomp-twin}
To measure $P$ and $Q$ along a learning trajectory we use antithetic twins.
A twin copy of the network is synchronised with the real one and then receives the
same weights and input spikes but the mirrored noise $-n$. With $s$ and $s'$ the
two scores, the even part $(s+s')/2$ estimates $P$ without bias and the odd part
$(s-s')/2$ estimates $Q$. Because the noise trace remembers past noise with decay
$\alpha=0.905$, a twin must mirror all noise still present in the trace: we run
three twins re-synchronised every $30$ steps with staggered offsets and decompose
each score with the twin that has run for $20$--$29$ steps, so that less than
$13\%$ of any unmirrored noise remains. Synchronising more often lets old noise
leak into the even part, which then contains part of the noise-reward
correlation (in our first implementation the two parts were spuriously aligned,
cosine $+0.5$). With the staggered twins the two parts are nearly orthogonal, and in
the update of the original rule the noise part is $3.6$ times larger than the drift
(Appendix~\ref{app:gridtoy-decomp}). The twins are a measurement device only:
behaviour and TD errors come from the real network.

\subsection{TD(\texorpdfstring{$\lambda$}{lambda}) for the two terms}
\label{app:decomp-lambda}
For the gradient term, Eq.~\eqref{eq:app-forward} is generalised advantage
estimation~\citep{schulman2016gae} with the actor trace decaying by $\lambda$
alone, i.e.\ $\lambda=\gamma\lambda_{\mathrm{GAE}}$: with an accurate critic,
$\mathbb{E}[\delta_{t+k}\mid s_{t+k}]=0$ for $k\ge1$, so $A^t$ is an unbiased
advantage estimate for any $\lambda$, and $\lambda$ trades critic bias against
return variance. $\lambda=\gamma$ gives the Monte-Carlo advantage $G_t-V_t$. Our
default ($0.8$ against $\gamma=0.5$ per environment step) lies beyond the
Monte-Carlo limit; it remains unbiased with an accurate critic but adds variance
and, with an imperfect one, a penalty $-(\lambda-\gamma)\sum_{m\ge1}\lambda^{m-1}V_{t+m}$
on future value estimates.

For the drift, the same argument shows that the trace is redundant in
expectation: $P^t$ depends only on the past, so with an accurate critic
$\mathbb{E}[A^tP^t]=\mathbb{E}[\delta_tP^t]$. During learning the critic is not
accurate, and the trace then integrates its systematic errors. In exploratory runs
of the toy task (Appendix~\ref{app:gridtoy-exploratory}), giving the drift its own
trace with decay $\lambda_P$ was consistent with both points: with $\lambda_P=0$ the
rule learned, but more slowly early in training than with $\lambda_P=\lambda$ or a
longer trace, and the difference shrank as the critic improved. For the drift, $\lambda$ is
therefore a timescale over which recent outcome trends weight the plasticity,
not a credit-assignment parameter. A further limitation applies to both terms:
the critic reads the perturbed network, so the baseline $V_t$ inside $A^t$ depends
on the noise it is meant to be independent of; for $\lambda=\gamma$ the gradient
term contains $-e\,D V_t$, a pressure towards lower value estimates wherever the
eligibility is high. We have not measured its size.

\subsection{Threshold adaptation}
\label{app:decomp-adapt}
For ALIF neurons the presynaptic factor
$\pi=\bar z-\beta\epsilon$ becomes negative when adaptation dominates, which
flips the sign of both terms for that synapse. In an exploratory measurement in the toy task this happens for
$20\%$ of the active synapse-steps ($24\%$ of input synapses), but these carry
only $1.1\%$ of the drift and $0.6\%$ of the noise part, because $\pi$ is small
when it is negative. At higher firing rates adaptation builds up further, and a
non-negative weighting of the gradient term (e.g.\ $\psi\bar z$) would avoid the
flip.

\section{A Network-Level Toy Task}
\label{app:gridtoy}

Single-neuron analyses (Appendix~\ref{app:trace}) cannot show how the
perturbation update interacts with learned readouts, recurrence and a critic. We
therefore built a small Snake-like task that keeps the properties of the
experiments that matter for the rule, small enough to run many variants on a CPU.

\subsection{Setup}
\label{app:gridtoy-setup}
The agent moves on a $5\times5$ board (four actions, softmax policy), food gives
$+1$ and leaving the board gives $-1$ and a respawn; it sees an egocentric
$5\times5$ view with a food and a wall channel ($50$ Poisson inputs, rate $0.5$
per step when active). The network follows the Snake architecture at a smaller
scale: a recurrent ALIF core of $36$ excitatory and $16$ inhibitory neurons, a
policy and a value field of $16$ neurons each, and linear leaky-integrator
readouts; every projection has a toroidal Gaussian fan-in of $12$
($\sigma=0.2$), and weights are initialised as in Appendix~\ref{app:arch}, mean
$\pm0.1/\sqrt{12}$ (inhibitory $\times3$) and standard deviation
$0.05/\sqrt{12}$, with the Dale sign imposed only at initialisation. Neuron
parameters, the noise ($\sigma=e^{-5}$), the regularisers and AdaBelief
($\beta_1=0.99$, $\beta_2=0.99999$, learning rate $10^{-4}$) are those of the
experiments; an action is taken every $K=10$ steps with $\gamma=0.5^{1/K}$ and
$\lambda=0.8^{1/K}$. Readouts always learn with their exact TD($\lambda$)
updates. All variants of a comparison run in the same process (five seeds,
$20$k environment steps), and we report the reward per environment step (a random
policy scores $\approx-0.13$). The same rule varies by up to
$\approx0.03$ between runs, so we compare only within a run.

\paragraph{Why random features fail.}
As in Snake, the initial weights are tiny relative to the threshold, so at the
start the field neurons are silent and a learned readout has nothing to read. A
reservoir (hidden weights fixed, readouts learning) stays at chance with silent
fields ($-0.126\pm0.005$, Table~\ref{tab:toy-decomp}), reproducing the Snake result that
the readouts alone cannot learn the task.

