\subsection{Exploratory analyses}
\label{app:gridtoy-exploratory}
During the exploratory phase we ran further checks in variants of this task with
scripts that are not part of the analysis package. We summarise their qualitative
outcomes, which motivated design choices but are not used as evidence; the
corresponding claims in the main text rest on the experiments of
Section~\ref{sec:results}.
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Estimates of the noise-free gate.} Running averages of $\psi$ worked and
were insensitive to their decay between $0.99$ and $0.999$ (a decay of $0.9$ let
recent noise into the average and learned more slowly). The gate at the noise-free
membrane $\psi(V-\eta_j)$, with $\eta_j=\sum_i\xi_{ji}$ the noise the neuron has
received, failed, because a noise-triggered spike leaves it with a reset the
noise-free neuron would not have had. A regression-corrected gate,
$\psi-\hat b(\eta_j-\bar\eta_j)$ with $\hat b$ the online regression slope of $\psi$ on
$\eta_j$, learned most consistently, but it needs the per-neuron noise sum, which the
implementation does not provide.
\item \emph{Gate width.} A gate narrower than the default (half-width $0.2$ instead
of $v_{\mathrm{th}}$) concentrated the drift at threshold, raised the activity and
slowed learning; wider gates performed like the default.
\item \emph{Ablations of the proposed rule.} Keeping only the drift ($g=\psi-\bar\psi$)
raised the activity and became unstable; keeping only the perturbation gradient
($g=-\bar\psi$) learned slowly; independent per-synapse noise, which weakens the
drift, learned less well; at three times the noise the original rule and the drift
alone failed with runaway activity while the proposed rule did not; replacing the
TD error of the hidden update by a constant of either sign or by a TD error with
random sign removed learning, whereas a TD error drawn from a recent random step,
which keeps its slow trends, did not.
\item \emph{Credit trace of the drift.} Giving the drift a trace of its own showed
that, with $\lambda_P=0$, the rule still learns but more slowly early in training
(Appendix~\ref{app:decomp-lambda}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Hebbian Structure of the Drift}
\label{app:hebb}

\paragraph{The objective of the drift.}
With Eqs.~\eqref{eq:app-forward} and~\eqref{eq:app-P-approx}, the drift term of
the update is
$\bar c\sum_tA^t\,\partial e^t/\partial W=\bar c\,\partial_W\sum_t
\mathrm{sg}(A^t)\,e^t$, where $\mathrm{sg}$ marks the outcome as a fixed
modulator (the derivative through it is the gradient term). Since the optimiser
descends, the drift minimises
\begin{equation}
L_H(W)=\sum_t A^t\,e^t ,
\label{eq:app-LH}
\end{equation}
the eligibility weighted by how much better than expected the outcome turned
out. With an accurate critic $A$ has zero mean, so $L_H$ is a covariance: the
drift moves eligibility, i.e.\ the sensitivity and plasticity of a synapse's
neuron, away from moments followed by positive surprises and towards moments
followed by negative ones. It allocates plasticity rather than optimising reward;
the gradient term optimises reward within that allocation.

\paragraph{Factorisation.}
Because the noise is shared per postsynaptic neuron,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:app-stein} suggests $P_{ji}\approx h_j\,u_{ji}$ with $u_{ji}$ a
presynaptic trace and $h_j$ set by the postsynaptic neuron. We tested this on
$3000$ steps sampled late in the training of a network with the proposed rule
(twin-estimated $P$ of the score with gate $\psi$, $2251$ synapses). With a free
postsynaptic factor per neuron and step, the factorisation captures $95\%$ of the
time-accumulated drift (cosine $0.953$; $62\%$ of single steps, which are dominated
by their own noise). Table~\ref{tab:hebb} shows the postsynaptic factor
by state: positive while the neuron is depolarised below threshold, negative at
the spike and during reset and refractoriness. A nine-entry table of this factor
times a presynaptic trace reproduces the accumulated drift (cosine $0.84$; $0.79$
when the table is fitted on one half of the data and tested on the other),
whereas the presynaptic trace alone ($0.54$), e-prop's eligibility ($0.42$) and a
spike-based Hebbian product, postsynaptic spike times presynaptic trace
($-0.01$), do not.

