\paragraph{Relation to voltage-based plasticity.}
Since the optimiser descends, the weight change of the drift is
$\Delta W\propto-\delta\,h\,u$. When $\delta>0$, presynaptic activity during
subthreshold depolarisation depresses and activity around a postsynaptic spike
potentiates, the sign pattern of the voltage-based rule of
\citet{Clopath2010Voltage}, here gated by the TD error and reversed when
$\delta<0$. The resemblance is one of structure, not of behaviour. In exploratory
runs (Appendix~\ref{app:gridtoy-exploratory}), the Clopath rule mapped to our neurons
silenced the network when used unsupervised and saturated it when modulated by the
TD error, and a noise-free rule built from the average postsynaptic factor of
Table~\ref{tab:hebb} also drove the activity up. The drift limits itself because it
is carried by the gate: when a neuron is silent or mostly refractory, $\psi$ is closed
and the drift vanishes, and it uses moment-to-moment information that an average
postsynaptic curve does not keep.

\section{The Empirically Found Rule}
\label{app:original}

The rule we first used, and with which the results of
Appendix~\ref{app:original-results}\todo{move the original Snake and Pac-Man
results here once the new-rule runs replace them in the main text} were
obtained, was found empirically: we explored variants of the update and kept
the one that learned best. It uses the gate $g=\psi$ and the uncentred score, and
the optimiser descends on $\sum_t\delta_t\,p^t$. By
Eq.~\eqref{eq:app-product} this descends on both terms: the drift with the sign
that makes it useful, and the gradient term against the return. It learned
because the drift carries the learning (Table~\ref{tab:toy-decomp}): in the toy task,
the drift alone learns as well as the full score, while the noise part contributes
nothing net and learns little on its own. The decomposition also explains the
behaviour of this rule in Snake. Reversing its sign fails, because the drift then
pushes the gated weights down and the network falls silent. Centring the score
on its running average removes only the slow part of the drift, which is mostly
fast, so the centred score still learns with the original sign. Without the gate,
$e=\bar z$ does not depend on the postsynaptic noise, so the decomposition
predicts that the drift vanishes and only the wrongly signed gradient term
remains, consistent with the failure of the rule without $\psi$. Table~\ref{tab:orig-snake} splits the original score into its running
mean and the remainder on Snake.

