\subsection{The Objective Horizon is Set by \texorpdfstring{$\gamma$}{gamma}, not \texorpdfstring{$\lambda$}{lambda}}
\label{sec:results-ceiling}

On Snake $5\times5$, the PPO reference makes the two performance metrics
concrete. With a long horizon ($\gamma=0.99$) the episodic and continuing
objectives diverge sharply (episode reward $\approx 22$ vs.\ reward rate
$\approx 0.24$, each poor on the other's metric), confirming that reward rate
and episodic return are genuinely different targets. At the short horizon the
spiking rules require ($\gamma=0.5$), both objectives collapse onto the same
compromise (episode reward $\approx 13$, reward rate $\approx 0.2$;
Fig.~\ref{fig:ppo}), because the
$\approx$2-step value horizon cannot represent either extreme. Crucially, the
PPO run with the spiking rules' objective (episodic, terminal mask on) lands
essentially where symmetric e-prop
does\pending{: symmetric e-prop number pre-rerun, now with episodic learning
signals; PPO itself unaffected}: a strong gradient optimiser given the identical
short-horizon objective reaches the same operating point as the local spiking
rule. The spiking results are
therefore limited by the objective horizon, not by their credit-assignment
machinery, and decoupling $\lambda$ (Section~\ref{sec:tdlambda}) can extend the
effective policy horizon only where the critic cannot already predict the
relevant rewards.

